## 2. Not MIT — Apple's declarations

Two things here are Apple's: the generated modules, and the API re-declarations that stand in for
frameworks Android does not have. Both exist only so that SwiftUI source compiles against Apple's
API; neither is a reimplementation of Apple's engine, and no Apple object code is redistributed,
decompiled or executed.

**`Sources/SwiftUI/Generated/`** — `SwiftUI.swift` and `SwiftUICore.swift` are the textual module
interfaces (`.swiftinterface`) that ship inside the iOS SDK, retargeted for
`aarch64-unknown-linux-android` by `scripts/port/retarget_interface.py` and expanded with
placeholder bodies by `scripts/port/inject_bodies.py`. Copyright Apple Inc.

**`port-shims/`** — Swift re-declarations of the public API (names, signatures, conformances) of
the Apple frameworks SwiftUI's declarations import: `UIKit`, `CoreGraphics`, `QuartzCore`,
`CoreText`, `Symbols`, `Spatial`, `os`, `simd`, `Accessibility`, … Transcribed from the interfaces
and headers Apple publishes, because with no Clang importer and no UIKit there is nothing to
retarget: the types have to exist as source. The *declarations* follow Apple's; the bodies,
comments and Android-side behaviour in those files are this project's and are MIT. Treat the
declared API surface as Apple's and the rest as ours.

## 3. Not MIT — OpenCombine (vendored, MIT of its own)

`Sources/Combine/` is [OpenCombine](https://github.com/OpenCombine/OpenCombine), pinned at commit
`1c6f02c7ed8140c0ba7a783aaddb6e0685a0037b` and compiled under the module name `Combine` so Apple's
declarations resolve unchanged. Its licence is MIT, **copyright (c) 2019 Sergej Jaskiewicz**; the
text is [`Sources/Combine/LICENSE.txt`](Sources/Combine/LICENSE.txt) and that file — not the
repository's `LICENSE` — governs that directory. The only transformation applied to the checked-in
copy is the mechanical `OpenCombine.` → `Combine.` module-qualifier rewrite described in
[`Sources/Combine/README.md`](Sources/Combine/README.md).

`Sources/COpenCombineHelpers/` implements the small C layer OpenCombine expects (unfair lock,
identifier, debugger hook) on top of pthreads, because Android has no `os_unfair_lock`. Its API
follows OpenCombine's, so it carries the same attribution; the implementation is this project's
and is MIT under section 1.
